   "line": [
    "b3",
    "e5"
   ],
   "spoken": "White opens with the Nimzowitsch-Larsen, the pawn to b3. The principled reply is e5 — the move that fights for the advantage rather than just equalizing.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "answer 1.b3 with e5"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 3,
   "t": 53.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/8/1P4P1/P1PPPP1P/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 2",
   "line": [
    "g3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 4,
   "t": 75,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/ppp2ppp/8/3pp3/8/1P4P1/P1PPPP1P/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 3",
   "line": [
    "d5"
   ],
   "spoken": "We occupy the center with d5. The formula against any flank opening is simple: take a sound chunk of the center and back it with pieces — it's not a race to cram in the most pawns.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "occupy and support, don’t over-push"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 5,
   "t": 89.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/ppp2ppp/8/3pp3/8/PP4P1/2PPPP1P/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 3",
   "line": [
    "a3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 6,
   "t": 130,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/ppp2ppp/5n2/3pp3/8/PP4P1/2PPPP1P/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 1 4",
   "line": [
    "Nf6"
   ],
   "spoken": "So we develop the knight to f6 instead of shoving more pawns forward and biting off more than we can chew.",
   "kind": "main"
  },
  {
   "ply": 7,
   "t": 145,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/ppp2ppp/5n2/3pp3/8/PP4PP/2PPPP2/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 4",
